    "abstract": "Climate action is shaped as much by politics as by technology and economics. The Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), central to mitigation and adaptation assessments, do not yet include a quantitative representation of political development. We outline a research agenda to systematically integrate political dimensions into climate scenario modelling.",
    "field": "Climate change politics",
    "problem": "The Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), central to climate modeling, lack quantitative representation of political development, leading to overly optimistic scenarios that fail to capture how political realities like institutional quality and conflict constrain climate action feasibility.",
    "method": "The authors propose a comprehensive research agenda to systematically incorporate quantitative political dimensions, such as institutions and conflict, into climate scenario modelling frameworks through an integrated, dynamic, and interdisciplinary approach to create more realistic projections.",
    "task": "The task is to propose a research agenda for systematically integrating quantitative representations of political development into the Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs) framework used for climate scenario modelling.",
